<h1>Quickstart</h1>
<p class="lede">
  Build es from source, start a broker on your machine, and write and read records with the CLI.
  Or let one command do all of it, including a restart, retention and compaction.
</p>

<h2>Before you start</h2>
<ul>
  <li>A Rust toolchain. The repository pins the version in <code>rust-toolchain.toml</code>; <code>rustup</code> installs it on first build.</li>
  <li>A free port on localhost. The examples use <code>9000</code>; the demo script uses <code>9876</code> (change it with <code>ES_PORT</code>).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Run the demo</h2>
<figure class="code">
  <figcaption><span>shell</span></figcaption>
<pre>make demo</pre>
</figure>
<p>The script runs these steps and stops at the first failure. It ends with <code>DEMO OK</code>.</p>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><p><strong>Reset.</strong> Deletes <code>./data</code> so every run starts the same way.</p></li>
  <li><p><strong>Build.</strong> Compiles the workspace in release mode.</p></li>
  <li><p><strong>Start the broker</strong> with <code>--segment-bytes 1024</code>, a deliberately tiny segment size, and waits for <code>/healthz</code>.</p></li>
  <li><p><strong>Produce 200 records</strong> into <code>orders</code>, two partitions. The small segments make several rolls per partition visible in <code>es topic describe</code>.</p></li>
  <li><p><strong>Consume through a group.</strong> Group <code>analytics</code> reads five records and commits offset 5.</p></li>
  <li><p><strong>Restart.</strong> Stops the broker, starts it again, and checks the group resumes at offset 5.</p></li>
  <li><p><strong>Retention and compaction.</strong> A topic with a 1.5-second retention loses its old segments; a compacted topic keeps only the latest value per key.</p></li>
</ol>

<h2>Run it by hand</h2>
<p>Start a broker in one terminal:</p>
<figure class="code">
  <figcaption><span>terminal 1</span></figcaption>
<pre>cargo build --release
./target/release/es-broker \
  --data-dir ./data \
  --bind 127.0.0.1:9000</pre>
</figure>
<p>Then create a topic, write a record and read it back in another:</p>
<figure class="code">
  <figcaption><span>terminal 2</span></figcaption>
<pre><span class="c-key">export</span> ES_BROKER=http://127.0.0.1:9000
./target/release/es topic create --name orders --partitions 2
./target/release/es produce --topic orders --key k1 --value <span class="c-str">'{"id":1}'</span>
./target/release/es consume --topic orders --partition 0 --offset 0</pre>
</figure>
<p>
  The record lands on the partition its key hashes to, so with two partitions it may be in partition 1.
  <code>es topic describe --name orders</code> shows each partition's end offset.
</p>

<aside class="note warn">
  <p>Authentication is off in these examples</p>
  <p>
    Without <code>--auth required</code> every caller is an admin. The broker therefore refuses to start that way on
    anything but a loopback address. Before exposing it, read <a href="<%= docs_api_path() %>#authentication">Authentication</a>.
  </p>
</aside>

<h2>Call the HTTP API directly</h2>
<p>Everything the CLI does is a plain HTTP call:</p>
<figure class="code">
  <figcaption><span>shell</span></figcaption>
<pre>curl -s -X POST localhost:9000/topics/orders/produce \
  -H <span class="c-str">'content-type: application/json'</span> \
  -d <span class="c-str">'{"records":[{"key":"k2","value":"hello"}]}'</span>

curl -s <span class="c-str">'localhost:9000/topics/orders/consume?partition=0&amp;offset=0'</span></pre>
</figure>

<h2>Run the tests</h2>
<p>
  The suite boots real brokers on ephemeral ports and drives them over HTTP, the binary protocol and the Raft transport:
  durability and recovery, retention and compaction, auth and quotas, consumer groups, replication and snapshot catch-up.
</p>
<figure class="code">
  <figcaption><span>shell</span></figcaption>
<pre>cargo test --workspace</pre>
</figure>
